const o=1,e="From Stone to Synaptic Intelligence: A Roadmap of Smart Materials from Early Civilizations to the Post-AI Era",t="Gyaneshwar Sharma, Anurag Chaudhari, Abha Singh, Pavan Kumar",a=[1,13],i=["1 Physics Department, Tilak Dhari Post Graduate College Jaunpur, U.P. India 222002","2 Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Datta Meghe Institute of Higher Education and Research","(DMIHER), DU, Wardha-442001, India","Corresponding author: GS* (gyaneshwar.jnu@gmail.com); PK† (pavank.feat@dmiher.edu.in)"],n=[{t:"h",x:"Abstract:"},{t:"p",x:"We, all living and non-living, are the successors and part of cosmic evolution. In this evolutionary track, we were nurtured and ruled by the almighty Nature. Our connection with nature was immersive and interactive in appropriate proportion. Since ancient times, sustainability has been a prime characteristics of almost all civilizations. Whole human civilizations are deeply intertwined within the capacity of material sciences. For thousands of years, the intention of introducing the material to elevate the standards of human lifestyle, even today we are expecting more from materials that have sophisticated performance beyond their boundaries. The civilization ages: Stone → Bronze → Iron → Silicon progression to AI ages, are hallmarked by the materials. This is the recognition and impact of power of material science in human civilization, which they are known by materials. However, this relationship is forever; now, the destiny of whole humanity lies in it. We have choice to choose evolution or destruction – everything lies in how we are responsible."},{t:"h",x:"1. Introduction"},{t:"p",x:"Our generation has witnessed immense growth in science and technology, which has upgraded our lifestyle and culture—from critical healthcare facilities to artificial intelligence (AI), from an era of subjection to mentorship. In this emerging phase of AI, smart materials could amplify AI’s heroic character to human life by adding an extra degree of freedom to devices. The exotic role of intelligent materials—like shape memory alloys and polymers, piezoelectrics, magnetostrictive and electrorheological-magnetorheological fluids, and self-healing materials—is highly responsive to minute changes in surrounding temperature, magnetic, electric, magneto-electric, mechanical, biological, or optical activity via changes in their physio-chemical properties (Yin & Rogers, 2022). Their potential is yet to be fully harnessed in medical, education, and defense technologies. As we march towards future Vikshit Bharat 2047, we will face unprecedented problems like resource scarcity, biological imbalance, hyper-connectivity, microclimate domes, digital well-being, ethical AI companions, virtual relationships, bio-implants, AI pets, cyborg enhancements, robot communities, sustainable food systems, and the challenges of climate change, including extreme heat, species extinction, and polluted skies. This future awaits us and could be harsh—especially if humanity does not recognize its responsibility and use technology wisely. Without strong philosophy and ethical practices, we cannot avoid these growing potential disasters. But ecologists mention that these outcomes are manageable if nations become determined to address the climate crisis, ecological imbalance, resource management, nature conservation, and extreme reliance on green energy systems. However, it needs strong cooperation and attention from the whole globe to passionately manage natural resources, economy, raw materials and waste management, and greener energy transition. But it will take urgent and unprecedented changes in how countries govern their economies, handle materials and waste, generate energy, produce food, use raw materials, and treat the environment. These aforementioned technology-driven crises are potentially furious and interlinked. From this perspective, smart materials have huge potential to play a legendary role in AI, climate change, pollution monitoring, medical diagnostic innovation, and energy solutions. As in the present scenario, sustainability is a focal point for the market of smart materials (Alon et al., 2025). This era is witnessing a shift towards the innovation of eco-friendly smart materials to serve humanity with outstanding potential products and designs."},{t:"h",x:"2. The Ancient Foundations: Dawn of Engineered Matter:"},{t:"p",x:"Material-driven technological advancement were emerged most significantly in Mesopotamian era due to its early mastery of metallurgy, invention of the wheel, and creation of writing systems. However, Egypt, China, and the Indus Valley also made significant contributions in construction, glassmaking, ceramics, and textiles. Each civilization excelled in different areas."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 1 Evolutionary track of Nature from civilization to sustainability"},{t:"l",x:["Table 1: Advancement of materials in progression of civilization","Civilization Key Material Innovations Distinctive Achievements","Mesopotamia","(Sumerians,","Babylonians, Assyrians)","Bronze metallurgy, copper and gold","use, invention of the wheel,","irrigation systems","Cuneiform writing, Code of","Hammurabi, wheeled vehicles,","advanced astronomy and mathematics","Egypt Stone masonry (pyramids), faience","glazing, glassmaking, papyrus, linen","textiles, goldsmithing","Pyramids of Giza, precision surveying,","medical papyri with surgical","knowledge"]},{t:"l",x:["Indus Valley (Harappa,","Mohenjo-Daro)","Fired bricks, standardized weights,","bead-making, metallurgy (copper,","bronze), textiles","Urban planning, drainage systems,","advanced bead and seal production","China (Shang, Zhou","dynasties)","Bronze casting, silk textiles,","porcelain, iron smelting","High-quality bronze ritual vessels, silk","industry, early steel production","Greece & Rome Marble and concrete, advanced","metallurgy, aqueducts, glassblowing","Roman concrete, aqueducts, Pantheon","dome, Greek bronze statues","Persia & Nubia Stone and brick construction, glazed","quartzite, ironworking","Royal Road, Nubian carbon steel","furnaces (among earliest high-temp","blast furnaces)","2.1. Stone Age: The First Toolmakers:"]},{t:"p",x:"The Stone Age is considered a monumental period of human evolution. Thanks to evolutionary period of 3.4 million years, humanity has observed 99% technical growth. Where humanity started use of stone as tool to weapon to meet their daily life requirements. The use of tools and weapons made from stone, bone and wood to collect their food had led a major impact on their nomadic culture to first settlements and agriculture development with featuring essentials development into fire, shelter, wheel cart and musical instrument (James Dilley, 2020)."},{t:"h",x:"2.2. Copper Age: The Genesis of Metallurgy:"},{t:"p",x:"In this age, human ancestors started relying on copper and gold metals to harness the food and ornamental purposes. Humans discovered the extraction mechanism to own these metals from their natural ore. In essence, the copper age is potentially considered as transitioning period from stone to Bronze. This is the age when metal came into ruling power (Aleksa Vučković, 2021). Where new cultures – characterized by a sedentary lifestyle - came into picture and new symbol of prestige and power were appeared. The culture that have scarcity of raw materials or lacked technological expertise, they immediately recognized its importance. They relied on trading with the community who have own mastery in this field. The landmark innovation in Copper Age was major milestone in the civilization with the socio-economic and political developments that shaped the future of generations."},{t:"h",x:"2.3. Bronze Age: Early Chemical Formulation and Alloying"},{t:"p",x:"To discover a stronger and more durable hunting tool, the bronze came into significance by adding tin to copper. Thanks to great metalworking skills in design, ranging from weapon design to other life-supportive articles like plows, axes, adzes and sickles, the agricultural farming and land became easier to manage, while increasing crop productivity. Bronze-made tools and weapons were superior to their counterparts. The impact of Bronze in ancient times was so remarkable and transformative period (captivating civilized society and transforming it into powerful nation) in human history that can be estimated – entire era was named the Bronze Age."},{t:"h",x:"2.4. Iron Age: Mastering Structural Transformation"},{t:"p",x:"As the Iron-made armaments were not likely inferior to bronze in terms of hardness and durability. However, Iron’s widespread availability around the parts of the globe and quality improvement from the discovery of steel – produced by heating iron with carbon - have gained significant reliability in terms of stiffness and durability."},{t:"h",x:"2.5. Roman Architecture: Self-Healing Concrete Working 2000 Years Later"},{t:"p",x:"The Pantheon's dome - without use of steel for structural bonding, is still physically continued. Thanks to its structural concrete that holds the structure firmly today (David L. Chandler, 2023). Old Roman roads are intact with same alignment even after 2,000 years. By the virtue its chemical composition, Roman concrete actually gets harder with time and exposure to weather. Romans blended volcanic ash and lime, made from raw quicklime, with seawater. When volcanic ash - pozzolana, and lime are exposed to seawater, the mixture forms aluminum tobermorite, which has self-healing characteristics and eventually strengthens with aging by the virtue of chemical bonding. In contrast, modern concrete has a lifespan of only a few decades. The Roman concrete based structures, whenever a minute cracks develop over the time, rainwater seeps in the developed cracks and dissolves chunks of limestone already present in the structure. This forms a calcium-carbonate solution that automatically seals the developed cracks through the recrystallization before they become visible with naked eyes."},{t:"h",x:"3. The Modern Era: (Post-World War II to Pre-World War III Era)"},{t:"p",x:"The two subsequent world wars have dealing between industry-academia institutions in association federal government funding agencies to invoke the authoritative global leadership. The aim of this collaboration during World War I is to drive innovative practices in the direction of medical science, chemical warfare, and aviation. During War I, Sonar (Sound Navigation and Ranging) device was developed to detect, navigate and map underwater objects by transmitting sound signal and analyzing their echoes. Where piezo-electrics are key ingredient of the Sonar. The piezoelectrics are the materials that produces electric signal on the application of mechanical vibration and vice versa. While, during world war II, there was technological shift from attack to defensive mode. These collaboration were intensified to strengthen defense mechanism – radar, tank, and extreme wiping out by nuclear weapons. Along with the multi-facilitated weapons, communication technology was one of the most elementary in order to manage communication gap between variety of forces and head quarter. Therefore, World War II was an indirect war of scientist, engineers, and technologist. During the War II, synthetic ferroelectric materials like barium titanate, lead zirconate and quartz crystal were developed in laboratory that have high piezoelectric efficiency in comparison to natural ferroelectrics."},{t:"h",x:"3.1. Polymer Era: Macromolecules and Tailored Macromolecular Properties"},{t:"p",x:"For millennia, humanity was totally relied on nature’s offering animal residue bone and leather, wood, stone, metal to fulfill their daily life needs. In the 19th century, the polymers were extracted from natural resources like plant-based, animal-based, and marine-derived. Plant based polymers have benefitted textile industry with their wider applicability caused by superior strength as well as sustainability and degradability. Animal derived polymers such chitosan are used as wound healer for antimicrobial and biocompatible properties. Marine based polymers are used in food coating and pharmaceutical encapsulation. Later. In 1920, L. Baekeland first time invented Bakelite - synthetic polymer, was create from phenol and formaldehyde, under high temperature and pressure environment."},{t:"h",x:"3.2. Silicon Age: Semiconductors as Computational Substrates"},{t:"p",x:"Late 20th and early of 21st century is considered as silicon age for their remarkable technological inputs. In essence, the silicon is the oldest companion of humanity and it have existence from earth crust to cosmic dust. Fig (2) is showing the crucial role of silicon based tool and materials. These materials have upgraded our lifestyle and community from tribes to virtual-reality. This period has emerged as noticeable growth in microelectronics and the digital age with hyper-connectivity. Silicon as a material is the main basis of current information technology. Silicon Valley, located at San Francisco, is a prime example. At present, San Francisco is the heart of semiconductor industry. Silicon’s outstanding physical properties have made the microelectronics possible. This has led us to the digital age and potentially benefitted various application sectors, including electronics, solar energy, construction, and healthcare. The MOSFET, or metal-oxide-silicon field-effect transistor, is a crucial part of the modern Silicon Age. It has been the most widely produced device in history. This revolution has reshaped technology, economy, culture, and thinking, may be continually leading to unprecedented advancements in human society (B. Maycock, 2025)."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 2 Pivotal role of Silicon-material in Human life"},{t:"h",x:"4. The Smart Material Revolution: Future Roadmap of Smart Material"},{t:"p",x:"Humanity has now recognized its responsibility to adopt noninvasive approaches for managing the non-renewable resources. Our carelessness has left a permanent imprint on our ecological systems. Sustainability is ultimate goal and we must not harm ecological balance. All stakeholders in society, industry, and federal R&D agencies are committed to a societal shift toward sustainability to promote eco-friendly products and designs. In this massive shift toward sustainability, the demand for eco-friendly materials derived from biodegradable polymers and renewable resources is becoming more pronounced. These materials are recyclable. Which can be repurposed for further use, thereby contributing to the circular economy and easing waste and resource management."},{t:"p",x:"Innovations in unattended care within healthcare technology have become more common. These technologies can change the entire healthcare sector with non-invasive, biodegradable in-vivo implants and better physiological monitoring to improve therapies and patient outcomes."},{t:"h",x:"4.1. Quantum Physics: The Emerging Era of Material Science"},{t:"p",x:"Materials’ characteristics are changed quantum mechanically, in their size, shape and dimensions, from the unusual to even truly strange. As technology emerged, materials transformed from bulky rock to sharp stone for tools and weapons, then to digital devices and quantum computing. Today, we design one-atom-thick quantum materials that are now essential to artificial intelligence. Materials' functionality has continuously advanced alongside shifts in their dimensionality, moving from the exotic to the truly strange. As technology evolved, materials progressed from rock and stone, to tools and weapons, to digital devices and quantum computing, culminating in atom-thick materials that now form a backbone of artificial intelligence. The classical computational techniques become impractical and incompatible due to requirement of exponentially huge memory to handle the number of atomic layers and interaction among them."},{t:"p",x:"Here, it becomes quite imperative to access the exotic features of smart materials on a prompt basis and at reduced cost. In this, quantum computing provides a paradigm shift in computational approach by exploiting quantum bits with their superposition and entanglement."},{t:"h",x:"4.2. Shape Memory Alloys: Thermally Actuated Phase Transitions"},{t:"p",x:"Shape Memory alloys are the class materials that remember its original shape. If intentionally or incidentally get deformed at certain temperature, their original shape can be restored via thermal treatment. The science behind a shape memory alloy's unique behavior is based on a reversible phase change between two different crystal structures, which happens when the material experiences temperature changes and mechanical stress. At low temperatures, the alloy is in the Martensite Phase. In this phase, it is soft, ductile, and can be easily deformed. Its twinned crystal structure can be rearranged under stress without breaking atomic bonds. When heated, the material transitions to the Austenite Phase, which is the rigid and symmetrical parent lattice structure that represents the material's memory shape. This change forces the atoms back to their original positions. The most commonly used shape memory alloy is Nitinol (Nickel-Titanium). Nitinol is technologically appreciated for its excellent electrical and robust physical properties, high corrosion resistance. In the biomedical area, Nitinol’s shows outstanding biocompatibility that makes it suitable candidate for self-responsive vascular stents, constant-pressure orthodontic archwires, and surgical utensils. Furthermore, their dimensional actuation capabilities—which efficiently transform thermal or electrical signal into massive mechanical force—have revolutionized whole aerospace engineering through morphing aircraft wings and adaptive satellite release mechanisms, while simultaneously advancing robotics engineering through the development of silent artificial muscles (Raheem et al., 2025). The integration of nanotechnology into smart materials is set to be the most resilient frontier in terms of responsiveness. Carbon nanotubes and graphene remarkably strengthen overall functionality while providing high strength and conductivity at ultralight weight."},{t:"c",x:"Figure 3 Controlling of various type of smart materials with external stimuli"},{t:"h",x:"4.3. Electroactive Materials"},{t:"p",x:"Soft electroactive polymers are gaining attention for their important role in the next generation of actuators and sensors. They are used in various applications, such as implantable biomimetic artificial muscles, wearable healthcare devices, soft robotics, prosthetics, morphing aircraft structures, haptic feedback interfaces, and epidermal electronics. Well-known electroactive materials include acrylic-based elastomers, silicone-based elastomers, and thermoplastic polyurethanes. Designers are using further interpenetrating network structures of these materials to improve their electroactive performance. Artificial muscles are especially important for mimicking skeletal muscles in bioelectronics. Understanding the role of AI in robotics, like the transformation brought by steam engines during the industrial revolution, AI and human researchers are moving away from tasks that require a lot of human labor to robotic systems that operate autonomously. (Maziz et al., 2017). Various heavy automobile industries already rely on robotics to perform their jobs. Humanoid robots, enabled with advanced microelectronics and materials integrated with artificial intelligence, will strengthen robotics, exoskeletons, and prosthetics by providing unprecedented functionality and performance that can closely interact with and assist humans. Commercially available exoskeletons already serve adults and people with disabilities to perform daily tasks. Intelligent designs of prosthetics are also helping as organ replacements. To meet end-user demands, significant improvements are still needed in these humanoids to make them more biologically realistic and practical for daily life."},{t:"h",x:"5. Advanced Applications and Technological Integration"},{t:"h",x:"5.1. Technological Integration Frameworks"},{t:"p",x:"The most appealing part of the smart materials is their ability to be seamlessly integrated into the matured technology in the direction of functionality and reliability to ascertain their role with high accuracy and autonomy. Additionally, they also provide real-time monitoring and situation-based responsiveness to end-users at an exceptional level. With highly sophisticated innovations in smart materials, the Internet of Things and artificial intelligence have become more versatile, from household applications to space missions. The integration of smart materials with advancements in nanotechnology and 3D printing is poised to enable highly customized, on-demand manufacturing technologies and user-specific products. In industrial settings, smart material-based structures can provide early warnings of malfunctions, so that maintenance costs and downtime can be minimized through their use."},{t:"h",x:"5.2. Smart Textiles: Adaptive Fabrics and Wearable Functionality"},{t:"p",x:"Smart textiles offer blending of both fashion –to fulfil the demand of modern lifestyle - and advance responsive functionalities, are engineered artifacts designed to exhibit responsive behaviors. When they are exposed to environmental or external stimuli—such as mechanical, electrical, thermal, chemical, or magnetic inputs. To achieve these technologically appealing activities, smart materials are integrated directly into fibers, yarns, and fabrics (Zhou et al., 2025). These textiles use responsive components like piezoelectric materials, shape memory alloys, color-changing substrates, conducting polymers, and hydrogels. These blended textile hold unique features for real-time healthcare and fitness monitoring. The smart materials are often embedded with seamless active sensors, actuators, and power sources. Textile companies can also take advantage of these innovations to connect designers and customized user demand, improving comfort levels and physiological feedback. Looking ahead, combining generative technologies with deep learning algorithms will be a key moment. It will push next-generation textiles toward more predictive and personalized care and real-time fitness management. The future merging of neuroscience and computing is expected to lead to the creation of complex, emotional, and aesthetically pleasing user experiences."},{t:"h",x:"5.3. Integration with 3D Printing: 4D Printing and Time-Evolutionary Geometries"},{t:"p",x:"The application of smart materials into 3D printing adds new dimensionality in term of performance, capability, durability, self-healing via enabling the structure as responsive and adaptive. Additionally, they can be programmed to change their properties in response to the environmental condition. The 3D printed designs may have more robust and resistant to wear and tear or structures for extreme conditions. As new smart materials will be explored, the emerging technologies with the integration of 3D printing would be more reliable and smart 4D materials."},{t:"h",x:"5.4. Autonomous Healing Structures: Synthetic Self-Repair and Longevity"},{t:"p",x:"Materials used as building blocks in structural development tend to degrade over time or when subjected to stresses beyond their limits. Repairing or replacing these materials takes a lot of time and can be expensive. To tackle this issue, biomimetic structures use sacrificial bonds that can reform after repeated fractures. These bioinspired structures replicate the self-healing mechanisms of natural systems—such as bone, silk, or nacre— offering a viable solution to technological failures. This provides immense strength to the design and structure, enabling autonomous healing without degradation or the need for external intervention. Such materials often have an important design feature: a stiff, bounded structure connected by thin, soft layers that contain sacrificial bonds for self-repair. Among the self-healing structures found in nature, bone is a particularly clever example. It consists of mineralized fibrils arranged in a stacked manner and held together by interfibrillar, non-collagenous proteins. (D’Elia et al., 2016)."},{t:"p",x:"Today, the Shahi Bridge—an ancient marvel of smart technology—continues to reflect its enduring importance even after more than 450 years in Jaunpur, U.P., India. The architecture and geometry of the Shahi Bridge—designed by Afghan architect Afzal Ali—demonstrate deep application of physics to achieve durability. Its modular system of multiple stone arches gives the structure flexibility. During the multiple severe earthquake, the bridge did not completely collapse and still in use. Instead, it absorbed seismic waves. However, only partial damage; about one-third, or seven arches, fell due to seismic fluctuation around 1934. Thanks to its modular design and construction, workers reconstructed the damaged sections to match the original design, while the main foundational piers stayed intact, today. Its remarkable strength against heavy river currents, repeated floods, monsoons, and earthquakes comes from the use of high-quality materials and smart structural design. A carefully optimized mixture was used to ensure long-lasting durability in a heavily flooding river environment. High-quality yellow/red sandstone (Vindhyan sandstone) used as the main material, combined with traditional lime mortar (chunam). The bulky sandstone blocks, which act as the main load-bearing elements, were selected for their chemical properties that protect the bridge from weathering and acid rain."},{t:"p",x:"The stones as building blocks were bound using traditional Mughal mortar blended of slaked lime/quicklime, brick dust (surkhi), molasses (jaggery), and organic binders such as lentils (urad dal) and tree resins. The overall durability of this mortar driven structure lies in its unique hardening eventually comes contact in water. Over many centuries, the carbonation yields it into a rock-hard limestone matrix with self-healing response, enabling it to absorb micro-cracks without crumbling further. To keep secure the sandstone against the violent force of the Gomti River, internal iron clamps were installed, sealed with molten lead to prevent water seepage and rusting."},{t:"p",x:"Self-healing based structure and design can automatically repair damage without any intervention of human attention. Technically, they resemble as bio-mimicking like microcapsules, reversible chemical bonds, and bacteria in concrete, and vascular networks in composites. These materials are versatile in civil infrastructure for the construction of heavy duty roads and bridges. They are also favorably applied in aerospace for aircraft composites and coatings, in electronics and energy for longer-lasting batteries and solar cells, and in automotive and consumer goods for scratch-healing paints and smart phone screens."},{t:"p",x:"Currently, most systems are limited to fill small cracks below 1mm. To activate healing process, they often require presence of heat or light to work that can be expensive and may have slow healing response. However, the field is have great potential in various application area. The construction of ever-lasting infrastructure and the urgent need for durable technology are driving factor of this area of research. The market is expected to reach $9.8 billion by 2030, with the greatest short-term impact in coatings, concrete, and batteries, and the potential for infrastructure that lasts over several centuries with minimal maintenance."},{t:"h",x:"6. Bioelectronics and the Medical Frontier"},{t:"h",x:"6.1. Bioelectronics: Merging Biological Systems with Synthetic Interfaces"},{t:"p",x:"Generally, implantation of sophisticated electronic devices with specific functionalities are required multiple major invasive surgeries. Today, bioelectronics community is examining alternative seamless medical procedure that are of less invasive methods using catheters and stents. This shift helps to reduce unnecessary tissue damage, shortens recovery time, and lowers long-term health complications. Recent advancements is being practiced over various organs of human body. These comprise ultrasoft endovascular neural probes for brain monitoring, optoelectronic pacemakers for heart therapy, and injectable conductive hydrogels that support tissue regeneration. By using smart materials like highly responsive shape-memory alloys, programmable polymers, zwitterionic hydrogels, and dynamic actuators, these next-generation medical devices create tissue-friendly interfaces without causing scarring. Which are designed to perform bio-mimic mechanical response to adjust with natural body movement, and decline their effect on immunity response to foreign implants. All these improvements aim to provide personalized clinical care. (Li et al., 2026). Material innovations in photovoltaics exemplify how the integration of smart materials can enhance both productivity and versatility, even in regions with limited sunlight, while simultaneously reducing costs. Similarly, we hope that smart material technology will prove to be a boon for humanity, as materials have been cooperating with human progress since the dawn of civilization."},{t:"h",x:"6.2. Healthcare Innovation: The Next Generation of Medical Diagnostics"},{t:"p",x:"The healthcare sector has enormous potential to adopt smart materials. Smart materials can provide exotic solutions to medical diagnosis by enhancing their efficiency, efficacy, and autonomous drug delivery due to significant innovations in transient materials for biocompatible devices (Simona, 2024). AI integration into medical devices is not about modernization — it is about survival. Shape-memory alloys provide impressive solutions as stents for minimally invasive surgery. Bioinspired smart materials can repair themselves after damage, which leads to greater longevity and sustainability. The use of responsive hydrogel materials in accurate drug delivery, particularly for insulin delivery, become quite reasonable so that patients can auto-administer and achieve precise drug delivery as per glucose levels in the body. These specialized hydrogels can alter their properties in response to a sugary experience in a reversible way (Elyse Glover, 2025). Piezoelectric-based wearables are used for real-time monitoring by converting physical activity into electrical signals. They can play a significant role in bioengineering and more accurate disease detection by providing high contrast and resolution in imaging techniques. Naturally occurring polymers like natural gums, peptides, polysaccharides – cellulose, collagen and proteins are smart materials."},{t:"h",x:"7. Global Macro-Drivers"},{t:"h",x:"7.1. Pioneering a Sustainable Energy Future"},{t:"p",x:"In the present era, the technologist have clearly understood that future technology and its diverse dimensionality lie in smart and intelligent materials. These materials have acquired a central position to provide sustainable energy solutions in different emerging fields e.g., energy, drug delivery, medical diagnosis, health care etc., as they can convert ambient energy into electrical signals – the most convenient form of energy. Various smart materials, such as photovoltaic cells and polymer gels, can transform waste energy into useful forms, while operating competently and minimizing environmental impact. Smart material-based designs show resilient responsiveness to real-time and environmentally triggered automation and auto-governed actuation. Lithium-ion batteries also help mitigate the intermittency inherent in energy generation and management of renewable energy."},{t:"h",x:"7.2. Market Value and Economic Insights: The Global Valuation of Intelligent Matter"},{t:"p",x:"The autonomous shift in role of smart materials, if they are subjected to the environmental change or external stimuli, which makes them more sensible in today’s technological approach. Due to increasing scope of the smart material products in aviation, defense, and medical industry, the market value of smart materials is projected to $179 Billion in 2035 from market size of $56 Billion from 2025, growing at CAGR of 12.17%. Asia-Pacific region has been identified as fastest-emerging marketplace for the smart materials. The recent innovations in energy sector are key driver of propelling market shift from piezoelectrics (largest market size by product) and shape memory materials (fastest-growing market) and fueling the demand of smart materials day-by-day to compete the race of energy autonomy among the Nations. The brightest part of this race is well aligned with sustainability and net-zero emission and deeply intertwined with the societal shift towards environmental responsibilities."},{t:"h",x:"8. The Beyond-AI Frontier: Future Challenges and Scope"},{t:"p",x:"The destiny of the blue planet and human future will depend on how aware we are about sustainability and practical measures. Climate change has been identified as a global emergency. This is alarming towards severe ecosystem disruption, erratic and risky weather patterns, and ultimately lead the collapse of public health. Extremely unpredictable weather and harsh climate shifts will reduce crop productivity and severely damage crop biodiversity, making farming incredibly harsh. Global crop yields are expected to drop abruptly, leading to widespread shortage and rise in food prices. If a temperature rise of 2°C, caused by global warming, is likely to push 189 million people into chronic malnutrition. Presently, over 2 billion people already at high risk of safe drinking water because of disrupted water cycles, mismanagement of ground water and prolonged droughts. Due to crop unproductivity, extreme heat, and flooding in coastal cities, international environmental agencies have threatened that hundreds of millions of people will be permanently displaced and forced to migrate by mid-century. Major coastal megacities and sea-level islands as the Maldives and Tuvalu will experience severe tidal flooding and complete loss of land for livings. If global carbon emissions continues, the life after 2050 will shift from manageable climate disruptions to non-manageable global crises. By the mid-21st century, the real world will be fully fused with the digital and physical worlds. As the digital realm expands, societal integrity will become fragile and there will be less room for genuine physical attachment and emotions between human beings. Ironically, humanoids will become more human-like. Cybersecurity will no longer remain an isolated technical discipline. It will become central to education, democracy, and even population management, making governance in these areas increasingly harsh. Major geopolitical blocs — such as Western, Eurasian, and Pan-Asian alliances — will operate on completely isolated, mutually incompatible network infrastructures."},{t:"h",x:"9. Future Roadmap of Smart Materials"},{t:"p",x:"After the domestication of animals, civilization has always sought to shift its work to its companions. Machinery innovation has solved many problems in industrial settings. Now, humans are taking the next step toward AI-humanoids to provide personalized care for tasks like household security, childcare, entertainment, and tutoring (N Goldstein, 2024). Personalized care means these systems use user-based learning and gain expertise through interaction. Furthermore, these humanoids would be networked with each other so they can share expertise and improve their skills collectively. Currently, we are at the threshold of the AI era, which will transform society and revolutionize industrial setups. However, this revolution will also bring inherent challenges where balance between humans and robots must be maintained. Addressing global future challenges will require multidimensional approaches, including the sustainable integration of smart materials through advances in computer science, materials science, socioeconomics, and policy-making."},{t:"h",x:"9.1. Concluding Vision: Matter with Mind"},{t:"p",x:"We are heading towards an era of materials that shift from passive and static—designed to meet specific property requirements—to smart and intelligent materials where certain functionalities can emerge automatically based on situation and environment. The ultimate realization of this journey is the transition from smart materials to truly intelligent materials, which have the potential to change the world of material design."},{t:"p",x:"Traditional materials research has relied on trial-and-error methods and labor-intensive testing. The rapid growth of AI has changed this approach, improving the accuracy and intelligence of materials science. This change boosts design productivity for specific roles. Their smart features can strengthen sensory networks, enable self-healing, and support strong, responsive designs. The inclusion of smart materials in design gives products the ability to respond intelligently.9.2. Timeline Beyond the Post-AI Horizon In the coming years, the AI would be ambient living assistance- as today we have electricity- and job-companion as human work-colleagues. Beyond the Post-AI Horizon, the 21st century unfolds as a transition from tool-based intelligence to ambient, self-evolving systems. From 2030 to 2040, AI will become essential and part of infrastructure and design, making cybersecurity a fundamental issue. By 2040 to 2060, the digital tribes, physical living, and biological worlds will be merged through versatile brain-computer interfaces and its global networking. The fragmented geopolitical groups would have their own isolated networks, and humanoids become progressively human-like and social-tribal connections will be asymptotically meaningless for human beings. Between 2060 and 2080, materials will shift from smart level to truly intelligent, putting self-decisive situational sentiments, and self-healing autonomy. Meanwhile, climate change stimuluses major issues for human lives such as mass migration for rehabilitation and food shortages. From 2080 to 2100, AI no longer relies on human and hijacks and operates within autonomously in closed loops. These situation force society to rethink what it means to be uniquely human at own level. By 2100 to 2150, future civilization will be at critical point: either humans, AI, and intelligent materials coexist and evolve together, or they split into separate techno-ecological islands where technology become essential for survival."},{t:"l",x:["Table 2: Timeline and Key Role of Material beyond AI horizon","Era Core Shift Key Impacts","2030-2040","Integration","Decade","AI become","infrastructure","AI embedded in living and non-living materials, Intelligent Grids","Cyber-security shifts from software to hardware-level defense","Humanoids gain dexterity but far-from reasoning.","2040-2060","Fusion Era","Digital + Physical","+ Biological merge","Brain-computer interfaces mainstream.","Education, democracy, population management become AI-governed."]},{t:"l",x:["Geopolitics blocs split into isolated network: Western, Eurasian,","Pan-Asian","Rise of Human-like humanoids vs decline in human attachment.","2060-2080","Intelligence","Transition","Smart →","Intelligent","materials","Materials gain situational awareness + automation self-healing","Design moves from trail-and-error to AI-driven prediction","Climate change: Mass Migration, crop failures, Coastal flooding","2080-2100","Post Human","threshold","“What can AI do?”","to “What is","human?”","AI self-evolves beyond human creation","AI run in distributed network","Humanoids indistinguishable from human in social roles.","2100- beyond","Symbiosis or","divergence"]},{t:"p",x:"Civilization forks Symbiosis: Humans +AI + Intelligence Co-evolve, scarcity drops, meaning become crisis"},{t:"p",x:"Divergence: Isolated blocs hardens, civilization fragments,"},{t:"h",x:"Materials design become survival tool"},{t:"p",x:"From the first flaked stone tools to self-healing Roman concrete, from copper alloys that built cities to steel that shaped the modern world, humanity's story is a story of materials. Each era was marked not just by what we made but by what our materials allowed us to achieve. We moved from using nature to manipulating it, and now to engineering matter atom by atom. Today, we stand at a new turning point. Smart materials no longer just respond; they sense, learn, adapt, and heal. When combined with post-AI systems, they are becoming extensions of our thinking—networks of matter that blur the line between material and machine. The pathway ahead is sophisticated. The future will not focus only on conventional substances but on intelligent materials who can think, collaborate, and grow and respond like us. The challenge now is to act sensibly. As we design materials with intelligence, we must adhere to sustainability, ethics, and balance in every bond and circuit of design. If the Stone Age taught us survival and the Bronze Age taught us transformation, we must remember that the Synaptic Age would teach us stewardship."},{t:"h",x:"References"},{t:"p",x:"Aleksa Vučković. (2021, April 3). The Copper Age: When Metallurgy Came to Rule the World. Https://Www.Ancient-Origins.Net/Artifacts-Ancient-Technology/Copper-Age-0015150. Alon, I., Haidar, H., Haidar, A., & Guimón, J. (2025). The future of artificial intelligence: Insights from recent Delphi studies. Futures, 165, 103514. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.futures.2024.103514 B. Maycock. (2025, May 15). Silicon: From Stone age to Phone age."},{t:"p",x:"Https://Sadiescience.ca/2025/05/15/Silicon-from-Stone-Age-to-Phone-Age/."},{t:"p",x:"David L. Chandler. (2023, January 6). Riddle solved: Why was Roman concrete so durable? Https://News.Mit.Edu/2023/Roman-Concrete-Durability-Lime-Casts-0106."},{t:"p",x:"D’Elia, E., Eslava, S., Miranda, M., Georgiou, T. K., & Saiz, E. (2016). Autonomous self-healing structural composites with bio-inspired design. Scientific Reports, 6(1), 25059. https://doi.org/10.1038/srep25059"},{t:"p",x:"Elyse Glover. (2025, April 24). https://www.bmrf.us/smart-materials-of-the-next-generation-driving-future-innovation/. Https://Www.Bmrf.Us/Smart-Materials-of-the-next-Generation-Driving-Future-Innovation/. James Dilley. (2020, October 1). 10 Interesting Facts About The Stone Age."},{t:"p",x:"Https://Www.Ancientcraft.Co.Uk/Post/10-Interesting-Facts-about-the-Stone-Age."},{t:"p",x:"Li, P., Hibino, N., Shi, L. L., & Tian, B. (2026). Minimally invasive bioelectronic implants. Nature Materials. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41563-026-02576-9"},{t:"p",x:"Maziz, A., Concas, A., Khaldi, A., Stålhand, J., Persson, N.-K., & Jager, E. W. H. (2017). Knitting and weaving artificial muscles. Science Advances, 3(1). https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600327 N Goldstein. (2024, June 28). The Coming Age of Humanoid Robots. Https://Raverai.Substack.Com/p/the-Coming-Age-of-Humanoid-Robots. Raheem, A., Mandal, K., Biswas, S., Ahari, A., Hassani Najafabadi, A., Farhadi, N., Zehtabi, F., Gangrade, A., Mecwan, M., Maity, S., Sharma, S., Arputharaj, J. N., Khan, P. A., Udduttula, A., Kouchehbaghi, N. H., Khorsandi, D., Vasita, R., Haghniaz, R., Herculano, R. D., … Manivasagam, G. (2025). Smart biomaterials in healthcare: Breakthroughs in tissue engineering, immunomodulation, patient-specific therapies, and biosensor applications. Applied Physics Reviews, 12(1). https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0238817 Simona, R. (2024). Commentary The Future of Smart Materials Applications in Health and Technology. https://doi.org/10.37421/2169-0022.2024.13.687"},{t:"p",x:"Yin, Y., & Rogers, J. A. (2022). Introduction: Smart Materials. Chemical Reviews, 122(5), 4885–4886. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.chemrev.2c00074"},{t:"p",x:"Zhou, M., Wu, J., Huang, L., Miao, M., Cui, Y., & He, X. (2025). Smart textiles: Sustainable, self-powered, portable, and durable wearable textiles for human health monitoring. Chemical Engineering Journal, 525, 170097. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2025.170097"}],r={n:1,title:e,authors:t,pages:a,affiliations:i,blocks:n};export{i as affiliations,t as authors,n as blocks,r as default,o as n,a as pages,e as title};
